00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:40.550
In 1900, the Fourth of July was an especially joyous, noisy affair.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:44.030
Everyone who could kept his carriage home that day.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:48.950
Fireworks were known to unnerve the horses.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:53.160
That summer, Americans were counting themselves.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.280
The 1900 census totaled 76 million of them.

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:59.830
There were now more telephones than bathtubs.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.980
still more blacksmiths than doctors. Only 8,000 cars, and in all the country, less than

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:21.640
10 miles of concrete roads. On July 4th, as tradition demanded, Americans proudly read

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:27.140
from the Declaration of Independence. But that summer, old-fashioned patriotism mingled

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.680
with old-fashioned politics.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.410
1900 was an election year.

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:36.000
The great question, who would lead the nation

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.400
into the 20th century?

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:13.780
In Washington, the last summer of the 19th century began quietly.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:18.370
Tourists moved through the city, guidebooks in hand,

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:23.580
wandering from one government building to the next.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:28.100
Washington was more like a small town than the capital of a great nation.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:40.920
so informal that one bureaucrat kept a hen by his desk.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:45.740
In June, President McKinley was meeting with advisers,

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:48.850
organizing the Republican convention.

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:52.050
The Democrats were already denouncing the war in the Philippines,

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.200
and McKinley was worried.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.860
In 1900, it was beneath the dignity of the president to campaign.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.000
McKinley would need a strength

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:13.130
strong running mate to take his case for an American empire to the American people.

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:20.300
On June 19th at the Republican Party convention in Philadelphia, he found him.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:25.220
As the governor of New York stepped forward to second McKinley's nomination, the delegates

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:26.210
went wild.

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:33.450
"He was all that the idolizing thousands wanted,"

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:35.150
one reporter noted.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.440
Direct, dashing, fearless, possibly a little careless,

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.240
and everything he was, the people liked him for.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:46.420
- Theodore Roosevelt is literally the rising star

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:49.730
of American politics in 1900.

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:53.170
He's gone within the space of a little over a year

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:56.670
from obscurity to being one of the most famous people

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:58.390
in America.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:04:59.180
He's a war hero.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:00.000
He's the war hero.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:04.380
THE SINGLE GREATEST WAR HERO TO COME OUT OF THE SPANISH-AMERICAN WAR.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.400
AND HE BECOMES THE MAN OF THE HOUR.

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.700
ROZOVELT'S CHARGE UP SAN JUAN HILL

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.870
HAD CAPTURED THE IMAGINATION OF THE COUNTRY.

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.220
ONE KANSAS NEWSPAPER MAN WROTE,

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:21.380
"THEODOR ROZOVELT IS THE COMING AMERICAN OF THE 20TH CENTURY."

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.980
WHEN ROZOVELT WON HIS PARTY'S NOMINATION FOR VICE PRESIDENT,

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.170
MCKINLEY HAD THE RUNNING MATE HE NEEDED.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:34.090
But as the delegates cheered,

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:37.600
a crisis was brewing halfway around the world

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.340
that would test McKinley's resolve

00:05:39.340 --> 00:05:42.430
to make the United States a world power

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:51.800
and challenge his hopes for re-election.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.190
Dear loved ones,

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:56.690
did you hear of the trouble the Christians are having

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:59.030
in the two provinces just east of us?

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.820
There is a native secret society called the Boxers, who say they are going to overthrow

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:09.070
the Protestant religion in China.

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:13.900
Just how far they will be allowed to persecute missionaries and their converts is not easy

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.120
to say.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:20.450
With love to you all, Eva.

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:26.150
Eva Price was a missionary from Des Moines, Iowa, one of thousands of Americans about to

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:30.000
be swept up in a brutal war that would rivet the attention of the country.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.680
the country all through that summer.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:38.260
One of the great ironies about 1900 is that the United States appears as one of the great

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:43.950
world powers at the very moment that the world is exploding into revolution.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:07:00.000
Ewa had arrived in China 11 years before to find an angry, frustrated people.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:08.800
their country carved up by hostile foreign powers bent on exploiting its vast markets.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:16.110
By 1900, China is looked at as a nation that cannot defend itself.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:21.200
China would be described as a bone among the dogs.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:27.320
The dogs are, of course, the imperial powers, and the United States is one of the dogs competing

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.000
for the bone.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.460
is seen as a great prize, as a bottomless market

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:39.440
for both industrial exports and for missionaries.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:42.020
- In 1900, thousands of missionaries,

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:44.120
inspired by the evangelical fervor

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.320
sweeping American universities,

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:49.810
were spreading the gospel in Asia.

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:54.820
China is open to the gospel now, missionary leaders said.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.500
It may not be so when she becomes strong enough

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:58.930
to dictate her treaties.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:07.140
Eva and her husband, Charles, had sacrificed a comfortable life in America to live in an

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:12.040
isolated province in China with their seven-year-old daughter, Florence.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:18.470
Eva was a very home-loving person. She had her family here, she loved them dearly, and

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:24.800
it was very hard for her to leave. She longed for letters from home and from her loved ones.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:30.000
She was very lonesome, but they wanted to do something for the betterment of

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.660
of the world and to spread Christianity was their way.

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:38.930
- Eva and Charles ran a school,

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:44.070
nursed the old and the sick, taught the Bible.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:46.830
Eager to record the exotic world around her,

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:54.740
Eva brought along a camera.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:57.340
Like most Americans, she saw the Chinese

00:08:57.340 --> 00:08:58.450
as a backward people.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.790
mired in superstition and magic, worshipping idols, waiting for the blessings of conversion.

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:15.710
The Chinese saw Eva as a barbarian, corrupting their ancient culture and religion.

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:19.910
"The missionaries destroy the gods we worship," protested the Chinese.

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:25.510
"They revile our ethics, ignore reason. Their aim is to engulf the country."

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:30.000
The Chinese couldn't figure out why the missionaries were coming into their land.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:37.540
the main word for them was foreign devils and that's what they called them foreign devils

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:43.200
with hatred of outsiders growing a mysterious sect began roaming the countryside

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:55.710
burning churches and killing missionaries they were called the boxers their rallying cry

00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:00.000
exterminate the foreigner their prime target

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:01.860
the missionaries.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:05.000
It is not surprising that the boxers

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.340
would target the missionaries.

00:10:07.340 --> 00:10:09.240
Once American missionaries were able

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.720
to get into interior provinces of China,

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:15.070
it opened these provinces up to American business people.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:17.630
And consequently, American diplomatic officials

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:20.410
on the scene nicely called the American missionaries

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:23.370
the pioneers for American trade and business

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:25.070
because once they opened up an area,

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:27.280
then the other Americans could come along behind

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:28.340
and sell goods.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:30.000
The Chinese did not miss this.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:37.720
By the summer of 1900, the boxer uprising had spread to the province where the Prices lived and worked.

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:47.910
Dear home folks, we are all well and as happy as circumstances will allow,

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:51.830
putting our trust in him for whose sake we are here.

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:59.370
Florence has quite a curiosity to see the boxers, but I hope she'll not get a chance.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:02.010
Love to you all, ever.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:48.650
All summer, the new fast transatlantic steamers were fully booked and had been for months.

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:59.600
75,000 Americans were sailing for Franks, teachers from Ohio, meatpackers from Kansas City,

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:13.200
firemen from Chicago, all bound for the 1900 Paris Exposition. Over 57 million people came

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:17.670
that year to see thousands of exhibits from every corner of the globe.

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:25.480
The world's fairs were a show place for the wonders of the present and the wonders

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:30.000
of the future. A place for every country, every product, every

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:38.090
nationality, every ethnic group, to show off. And you got a sense that the world was a bit

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:50.510
smaller, and America was very much part of it. American businessmen found the exposition a

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:55.190
glorious opportunity to hawk everything from linotype machines to processed meats.

00:12:55.190 --> 00:13:00.000
The Americanization of the world, an English writer noted, is

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:08.010
well underway.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:12.360
Paris Exposition was a tribute to technology and progress.

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:15.360
Visitors marveled at moving sidewalks.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:20.440
Wireless telegraphy, the most powerful telescope ever built,

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:24.410
first escalator anyone had ever seen.

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:27.200
At night, the city was ablaze.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:30.000
An electric searchlight was said to be like the finger

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.730
of some sun god the conscious intent of the people designing the world's fairs is clearly

00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:42.770
to teach people that progress works so the hall of electricity the electric light displays

00:13:42.770 --> 00:13:47.150
are terrific things fountains with different colored lights in them and dazzling stuff

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:52.190
call them wonderments the sorts of things where you just walk around shaking your head and say

00:13:52.190 --> 00:14:00.000
what will they think of next you also have scenes of colonial superiority from india and

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.440
from Africa and places like this that are steeped in a kind of a white man's superiority

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:06.720
racist sort of a thing.

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:12.340
Let's say, come here and you'll really see what progress is doing because you will see

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:19.620
the counterpoint to progress, which is backward, savage, uncivilized, interesting, salacious

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:22.120
stuff.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:38.410
Each World's Fair cataloged in hierarchical rank peoples of the world.

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:48.910
And the white folk were always at the top, and the people of color far at the bottom.

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:56.430
People from the Philippines or from Africa were displayed as examples of the brutish

00:14:56.430 --> 00:15:00.000
savagery that highlighted the essential goals.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:21.650
the goodness by contrast of white Americans and white Europeans.

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:26.510
In 1900, the belief that people of color were inferior to white people

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:29.920
was as widespread in the United States as any place in the world.

00:15:31.850 --> 00:15:34.710
Popular songs, theater, vaudeville,

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:37.710
all portrayed African Americans as ignorant,

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:39.670
foolish, and childlike.

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:44.260
- American culture was so racist

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:49.170
and thought of African Americans as inferior

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:52.170
as a matter of course.

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:55.190
We were depicted as buffoons,

00:15:55.190 --> 00:16:00.000
oftentimes eating watermelon with huge red lips

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.850
and large eyes.

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:06.890
And they essentially made African Americans ridiculous.

00:16:06.890 --> 00:16:11.130
This kind of racism was kind of a paternalism.

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.950
Yes, these people are inferior.

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:17.070
They will never be as great as we are,

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:24.210
but we can help them.

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:25.810
- Although many black Americans

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:30.000
were doctors, ministers, teachers,

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:34.610
Most found themselves trapped in a hostile world.

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:44.890
Nine out of ten still lived in the South,

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:47.000
where they were denied the freedoms

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.000
that other Americans took for granted,

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:51.980
even the right to vote.

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:55.800
By 1900, we had no rights.

00:16:55.800 --> 00:17:00.000
We were not treated like sex.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:01.900
second-class citizens.

00:17:01.900 --> 00:17:05.550
We were treated as non-citizens.

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:10.900
After the Civil War, African Americans had voted, sent representatives to Congress,

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:16.760
served as sheriffs, justices of the peace, and sat alongside whites on juries, school

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:19.620
boards, and city councils.

00:17:19.620 --> 00:17:23.540
But by 1900, all that had come to an end.

00:17:23.540 --> 00:17:30.000
Through poll taxes, literacy tests, and dozens of other schemes, Southern whites had stripped

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.930
African-Americans of their most basic rights as citizens.

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:38.990
- By 1900, the South was what we called Jim Crowed.

00:17:38.990 --> 00:17:42.290
That meant that it was segregated.

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:46.200
We were not going to be seated next to whites.

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:51.000
We couldn't use the same facilities as whites.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:55.140
And to make sure that African-Americans did not attempt

00:17:55.140 --> 00:18:00.000
to assert themselves in any way, the white South

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.770
resorted to terrorism.

00:18:03.770 --> 00:18:08.610
The last 16 years of the 19th century,

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:22.240
there were 2,500 lynchings in the South.

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:25.000
The majority of them were African-Americans.

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:42.030
and that terrorism reached an intensity in 1900.

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:48.170
Early that year, Representative George White of North Carolina

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:53.300
had put a bill before Congress to make lynching a federal crime.

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:58.440
Not long before, there had been 22 black congressmen in Washington,

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:00.000
now in both the House and Senate.

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:03.750
George White was the only one who remained.

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:08.420
To cheapen Negro life, he said, is to cheapen all life.

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:12.000
The first murder paves the way for the second

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:20.870
until crime ceases to be abhorrent.

00:19:20.870 --> 00:19:25.370
There had been 107 reported lynchings in 1899,

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:27.400
White told the House.

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:29.800
His bill was designed to stop the killing.

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:34.440
George White's anti-lynching bill really didn't have a chance.

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:41.510
A bill by the last remaining African-American congressman was not going to get anywhere.

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:47.740
Whites in Congress, many of them from the South, said,

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:54.320
we need to keep the vicious, brutal African-American males in line

00:19:54.320 --> 00:19:59.160
to keep them from attacking the flower of white womanhood.

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:07.480
With terror and oppression part of daily life, America's nine million blacks looked to one

00:20:07.480 --> 00:20:11.180
man more than any other for leadership.

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:16.520
Booker T. Washington was just finishing his autobiography, "Up From Slavery," which would

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:21.430
inspire generations of black Americans.

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:30.000
Washington had worked his way from being a slave to being a coal miner as a little boy, to working for

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:36.580
for a wealthy white woman to setting up this school in Tuskegee.

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:41.240
Back in 1881, Washington had founded Tuskegee Institute,

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:45.910
dedicated to teaching black Americans practical skills.

00:20:45.910 --> 00:20:50.840
Our knowledge, he said, must be harnessed to the real things in life.

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:54.570
He was advocating for African Americans to be carpenters,

00:20:54.570 --> 00:21:00.000
to be bricklayers, to be seamstresses, to create black businesses.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:05.310
and to become a self-reliant people.

00:21:05.310 --> 00:21:08.490
Booker T. Washington was an accommodationist,

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:13.650
and his program was to accommodate the social

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:18.060
and political situation of the South.

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:22.480
Washington felt that if you don't have economic power,

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:27.050
then you're going to the white man empty-handed.

00:21:27.050 --> 00:21:30.000
Black progress, Washington said, must be

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:39.410
laid on love of work, economy, ownership of property, bank accounts. But in 1900, a new

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:47.180
black leader was emerging, unwilling to compromise with white America. W.E.B. Du Bois believed

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:52.000
that without political power, African Americans would never achieve equality.

00:21:52.000 --> 00:22:00.000
W.E.B. Du Bois did not feel that you could accommodate injustice. He felt that if you

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.440
You didn't demand your rights as other citizens.

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:07.030
You were really selling your people short.

00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:09.790
Du Bois never intended to be an activist.

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:12.720
He wanted to be a scholar.

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:18.990
Born free in a small New England town, he had studied Greek and Latin as a boy and became

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:23.610
the first black to receive a Ph.D. from Harvard.

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:30.000
By the spring of 1899, Du Bois was a 31-year-old professor of sociology at Atlanta.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:36.240
a university. He believed he could fight bigotry and hatred with reason and scholarship.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:42.900
Du Bois believed in the power of social science to transform the society. He kept saying,

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:46.360
"Americans are thinking wrong about race because they don't know."

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:53.910
But then Du Bois witnessed a crime so vicious, it convinced him that writing alone was not

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:57.140
enough to overcome the prejudice of white Americans.

00:23:03.840 --> 00:23:17.420
In Atlanta, in April 1899, Sam Hose, an African American, was lynched, burned, then brutally dismembered, and put on display in a downtown store window.

00:23:17.420 --> 00:23:25.580
And Du Bois passed the storefront in which the dismembered parts of Mr. Hose were on display.

00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:30.000
He stopped, riveted to the spot.

00:23:30.920 --> 00:23:38.590
went back to the campus and decided that social science wouldn't transform society, that politics

00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:46.440
would. Dubois becomes convinced that the cure for society's ills was not to tell people

00:23:46.440 --> 00:23:54.010
the truth, but to convince them to act upon that truth. Dubois would soon break openly

00:23:54.010 --> 00:24:00.000
with Booker T. Washington. Mr. Washington, Dubois would write, asks black

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:05.660
people to give up political power, civil rights, higher education of Negro youth.

00:24:06.780 --> 00:24:12.990
But Negroes must insist that voting is necessary for modern manhood, that discrimination is

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:17.990
barbarism, that black boys need education as well as white boys.

00:24:17.990 --> 00:24:25.120
The problem of race, he said in 1900, would be the problem for the 20th century.

00:24:48.810 --> 00:24:51.200
All through the spring and early summer,

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:55.610
Americans were reading that the Boxer Rebellion had spread.

00:24:55.610 --> 00:24:57.980
More churches had been destroyed.

00:24:57.980 --> 00:24:59.890
More missionaries killed.

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:10.710
We have heard, Ewa Price wrote, that the boxers are in our own city.

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:17.480
We have learned what it is to have the feeling that we are facing death itself.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:22.970
300 miles north of the prices in Peking, the capital of China,

00:25:22.970 --> 00:25:27.200
the compound where hundreds of foreign diplomats lived had come under siege.

00:25:31.880 --> 00:25:40.200
Among those trapped inside were Americans and their families, surrounded by more than

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:44.150
20,000 angry Chinese.

00:25:44.150 --> 00:25:48.120
They advanced in a solemn mass, wrote one hostage.

00:25:48.120 --> 00:25:54.560
Their yells were deafening, while the roar of gongs, drums, and horns sounded like thunder.

00:25:54.560 --> 00:26:00.000
And everyone understands that if this siege succeeds, that they are probably going to massacre

00:26:00.190 --> 00:26:03.750
all of the foreigners that they can get their hands on.

00:26:03.750 --> 00:26:06.750
On June 13th, the boxers cut the telegraph lines

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:16.810
connecting the foreign compound with the rest of the world.

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:19.150
With American soldiers already dying

00:26:19.150 --> 00:26:21.750
in an unpopular war in the Philippines,

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:24.150
McKinley was being forced once again

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:27.200
to take action on the other side of the globe.

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:30.000
McKinley has to make a historic decision,

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:35.500
His decision is whether or not to send U.S. troops out of Manila and onto the mainland

00:26:35.500 --> 00:26:36.880
of Asia.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:42.360
And what McKinley does is not only order the troops onto the Asian mainland to fight in

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:45.440
China, but he does it without consulting anyone.

00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:50.110
He essentially goes to war without asking Congress anything about it.

00:26:50.110 --> 00:26:54.810
And it becomes a very important historic precedent that later American presidents will use to

00:26:54.810 --> 00:26:56.820
order American troops around the world.

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:06.880
By the end of June, 2,500 American soldiers had left Manila to join an international army.

00:27:08.350 --> 00:27:12.770
Their mission? Put an end to the Boxer Rebellion and liberate the hostages.

00:27:16.340 --> 00:27:20.290
Far from Peking, the prices faced increasing danger.

00:27:22.550 --> 00:27:26.110
We're living in a suspense that cannot be imagined, ever wrote.

00:27:26.110 --> 00:27:29.190
We live moment by moment.

00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:30.000
A killer heat wave hit America that summer, holding most of the nation in its grip.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:54.450
In the Great Lakes, summer meant escaping to the shore, if only for a day.

00:28:54.450 --> 00:28:58.260
Never before, vacations were becoming a part of American life.

00:29:25.920 --> 00:29:29.220
the wealthy spent their summers in exclusive enclaves.

00:29:32.590 --> 00:29:38.940
In 1900, there were 4,000 millionaires, and with no income tax, a million dollars went

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:39.890
a long way.

00:29:39.890 --> 00:29:54.740
The rich idled their summers away, living much as kings would, if kings had the money.

00:29:54.740 --> 00:30:00.000
By 1900, the great American fortunes far surpassed the fortunes of the

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:05.520
leading dukes and lords and royal heads of Europe in the summers these people

00:30:05.520 --> 00:30:11.410
went to Newport to Southampton to Maine and they built fabulous luxurious

00:30:11.410 --> 00:30:24.530
chateaus especially in Newport that was the fashionable colony there were ornate

00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:29.480
palaces with over a hundred rooms called cottages

00:30:31.660 --> 00:30:33.560
and competitions to see who could hold

00:30:33.560 --> 00:30:36.810
the most outrageous party.

00:30:36.810 --> 00:30:39.670
One host invited his guests to bring their horses

00:30:39.670 --> 00:30:44.160
to a sit-down dinner.

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:47.320
- People gave elaborate costume balls

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:48.980
in which at one of them,

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:50.540
there were something like 14 people

00:30:50.540 --> 00:30:54.030
who came as Louis XVI.

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:58.420
There were lots of Napoleons and a few Marie Antoinettes.

00:31:05.690 --> 00:31:11.750
In small towns that summer, there were picnics, sports, fireworks.

00:31:33.160 --> 00:31:43.130
music of john philip susa john philip susa was everywhere in 1900 and band music was everywhere

00:31:43.130 --> 00:31:47.270
there weren't too many middle class people in america listening to beethoven because how could

00:31:47.270 --> 00:31:54.030
they there were no recordings yet they couldn't go to the concert halls the philharmonic was not

00:31:54.030 --> 00:32:00.000
accessible band music on tour the concert in the park was cooking in 1900 people loved

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:08.140
loved it. John Philip Sousa was the Arthur Fiedler of his time but didn't have

00:32:08.140 --> 00:32:11.140
any strings he didn't have any violins he couldn't do a lot of things he didn't

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:17.730
have any pianists he had bands horns I mean you had to be heard across the park

00:32:32.620 --> 00:32:38.940
Sousa was king, but in 1900, outdoor band concerts had a new kind of competition.

00:32:42.170 --> 00:32:51.790
Americans everywhere were listening to records, buying sheet music and pianos, and singing

00:32:51.790 --> 00:32:52.790
the same songs.

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:56.520
The music industry had just been born.

00:32:57.100 --> 00:32:58.940
Songs had become commodities.

00:33:00.380 --> 00:33:04.090
It was possible to publish a tune in New York on Friday

00:33:04.090 --> 00:33:07.650
and have copies of it in San Francisco next week,

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:12.080
not to mention Des Moines and everywhere in between.

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:16.650
The Five and Ten, that's where the music was merchandised.

00:33:16.650 --> 00:33:21.500
Rural free delivery, RFD, kicked in in 1897

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:24.130
so the farmers could get some music.

00:33:24.130 --> 00:33:28.230
Here was finally a business, an industry.

00:33:28.230 --> 00:33:30.000
There's money to be made,

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:33.060
And out of the woodwork come all of these young dudes saying,

00:33:33.180 --> 00:33:35.680
Hey, man, let's go into the music business.

00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:38.240
I don't know anything about it. Never mind.

00:33:38.740 --> 00:33:40.020
We can sell sheet music.

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:43.730
They sold a million copies in Chicago last week.

00:33:44.570 --> 00:33:45.050
Let's go.

00:33:51.940 --> 00:33:57.600
In 1900, a new hit was flying off the racks of five-and-dime stores all across the country.

00:33:58.290 --> 00:33:59.430
The Maple Leaf Rag.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:00.600
Scott Joplin.

00:34:01.260 --> 00:34:05.920
1899, late in the year, Scott Joplin's first successful rag,

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:08.600
the Maple Leaf Rag, is published out in Sedalim, Missouri.

00:34:09.390 --> 00:34:12.020
In 1900, it snowballed.

00:34:12.260 --> 00:34:13.540
It sold and sold and sold,

00:34:13.620 --> 00:34:16.040
and it's probably the best-selling rag of all time.

00:34:17.490 --> 00:34:20.170
Joplin had learned classical music from his father,

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:23.090
a former slave who was an accomplished violinist,

00:34:23.090 --> 00:34:27.490
and the syncopated style of plantation songs and dances

00:34:27.490 --> 00:34:30.000
from his mother, played the banjo,

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:31.360
and sang in church choirs.

00:34:32.920 --> 00:34:35.240
Ragtime is not an unsophisticated music.

00:34:36.250 --> 00:34:38.030
Rhythmically, it's complex.

00:34:38.770 --> 00:34:40.650
The left hand keeps a constant beat.

00:34:41.160 --> 00:34:43.980
The right hand is constantly going off that beat.

00:34:46.160 --> 00:34:50.790
And when it does that continuously,

00:34:51.090 --> 00:34:52.970
it creates an elation.

00:34:55.610 --> 00:34:59.650
I can't explain why that is,

00:34:59.730 --> 00:35:00.000
but in that sense,

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:05.430
hands of someone who knows how to do it, it's exciting.

00:35:05.430 --> 00:35:10.940
In 1900, Joplin was 32 years old and had spent most of his life traveling around the

00:35:10.940 --> 00:35:18.390
Midwest making music with other African-American musicians, the musicians who invented ragtime.

00:35:18.390 --> 00:35:23.650
In 1900, you have these very talented young black musicians who are now absorbing all

00:35:23.650 --> 00:35:30.000
the music that's around them. They heard French quadrilles, they heard Baptist hymns, plus

00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:36.240
the European masters. They heard Chopin, they heard Mendelssohn. But because it came from

00:35:36.240 --> 00:35:42.970
the black world, the whole thing was considered as gross, an intrusion, bad.

00:35:42.970 --> 00:35:49.400
This invasion of vulgarity in music is a national calamity, wrote one critic. This plague is

00:35:49.400 --> 00:35:54.890
upon us like an epidemic of cholera. The people have sold themselves body and soul to the

00:35:54.890 --> 00:36:00.000
musical Satan. Alas, this is what sells.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:05.180
to take some time for the pianists, not to mention the ears of Americans, to say, "What

00:36:05.180 --> 00:36:12.630
is that? Wow, I like that. What are they playing, Harriet? I mean, it's exciting. What do they

00:36:12.630 --> 00:36:15.810
call that? Oh, they call it rag time."

00:36:30.410 --> 00:36:43.820
In China, the crisis grew more ominous as the siege in Peking continued.

00:36:43.820 --> 00:36:52.120
Inside the foreign compound, 480 men were holding off 20,000 Chinese with just five cannons.

00:36:52.120 --> 00:36:57.100
Seven Americans had been killed, many more wounded.

00:36:57.100 --> 00:37:00.000
Supplies were dwindling.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:07.280
The diplomats and their families were surviving on rice and the meat from slaughtered horses.

00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:13.660
American counsel Edwin Conger managed to smuggle out one last desperate message: Quick relief

00:37:13.660 --> 00:37:22.310
only can prevent General Massacre.

00:37:22.310 --> 00:37:27.860
But American soldiers were still many miles from Piquet, fighting their way through the

00:37:27.860 --> 00:37:30.000
countryside.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:37.480
Welsh Fusiliers, Bengal Lancers, Punjabis, Sikhs, Russians, Japanese, French, Italians.

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:44.640
The United States had never sent a military force outside the Western Hemisphere until

00:37:44.640 --> 00:37:45.640
1898.

00:37:45.640 --> 00:37:53.470
Suddenly, in 1900, we have 5,000 American troops on the mainland of Asia, fighting in

00:37:53.470 --> 00:37:56.940
league with the European powers.

00:37:56.940 --> 00:38:00.000
This was something that was unimaginable.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:08.880
"If only soldiers would come into our province," Everprice wrote home, "we would probably

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:11.240
be saved.

00:38:11.240 --> 00:38:16.270
We have so longed and prayed they may come in time."

00:38:16.270 --> 00:38:21.460
They were virtually trapped because they were in the interior, and there really was

00:38:21.460 --> 00:38:23.910
almost no way out.

00:38:23.910 --> 00:38:28.200
And all they could hope for really was that Allied soldiers would come soon.

00:38:43.660 --> 00:38:48.780
On July 5th, Theodore Roosevelt, campaigning in the Midwest, defended McKinley's decision

00:38:48.780 --> 00:38:51.480
to send troops to China.

00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:56.080
America's new role as a world power demanded it, he said.

00:38:56.080 --> 00:39:00.000
As for the war in the Philippines, we should hold on to that island nation.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:07.050
at any cost. "I am for expansion every time," Roosevelt told the crowds. "I do not want

00:39:07.050 --> 00:39:14.970
this flag to come down where our men have fought and shed their blood for it."

00:39:14.970 --> 00:39:20.330
That same day, from his home in Lincoln, Nebraska, William Jennings Bryan, the Democratic candidate

00:39:20.330 --> 00:39:27.090
for president, challenged the Republican Party's vision of a more militant America.

00:39:27.090 --> 00:39:30.000
Four years before, Bryan had also won the nomination.

00:39:30.220 --> 00:39:34.680
and gone down to defeat calling McKinley a pawn of big business.

00:39:34.680 --> 00:39:38.320
Now he labeled the president an imperialist.

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:41.070
"Imperialism," Bryan told reporters,

00:39:41.070 --> 00:39:45.090
"is the most dangerous of the evils now menacing our country."

00:39:45.090 --> 00:39:48.630
This was the time when the Philippine insurrection was going badly.

00:39:48.630 --> 00:39:52.750
This was the time when the boxers were besieging the foreign compound,

00:39:52.750 --> 00:39:55.980
and we did not know whether or not they were going to massacre

00:39:55.980 --> 00:39:57.200
all of the foreigners in China.

00:39:57.200 --> 00:40:00.000
And it looked as though McKinley's foreign policy

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:05.600
policies were in shambles.

00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:10.520
All summer, Brian drew huge crowds across the country as he lashed out at the president

00:40:10.520 --> 00:40:12.840
for trampling the rights of the people of Asia.

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:19.890
"We dare not educate the Filipinos," Brian said, "lest they learn to read the Declaration

00:40:19.890 --> 00:40:23.600
of Independence and the Constitution of the United States."

00:40:23.600 --> 00:40:27.410
Brian believes that American foreign policy is immoral and that the United States has

00:40:27.410 --> 00:40:30.000
no business fighting these kinds of wars.

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:34.580
He believes that what McKinley has done is not only corrupt American society,

00:40:34.580 --> 00:40:37.990
but has corrupted the American Constitution.

00:40:37.990 --> 00:40:45.560
While McKinley managed his campaign from behind the scenes in Canton, Ohio,

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:48.240
Roosevelt answered Bryan's charges.

00:40:48.240 --> 00:40:52.000
Theodore Roosevelt was the best possible spokesman

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:57.070
McKinley could have found to make the imperialist case in 1900.

00:40:57.070 --> 00:41:00.000
He was the symbol of American imperialism.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:05.340
imperialism, the rough rider of the war of 1898, the person who had written more about

00:41:05.340 --> 00:41:10.490
the glories of American expansionism than probably any other person at that time.

00:41:10.490 --> 00:41:16.460
Every expansion of a great civilized power, Roosevelt wrote, means a victory for law,

00:41:16.460 --> 00:41:18.540
order and righteousness.

00:41:18.540 --> 00:41:23.460
The Democrats attacked the Republicans on the grounds that they were using force to

00:41:23.460 --> 00:41:30.000
impose American society on other races in the Philippines and in China.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:32.460
Roosevelt thought this was absurd.

00:41:32.460 --> 00:41:36.260
Roosevelt pointed out that this is exactly what we had done with the Indians, and he

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:43.000
hadn't known too many Democrats who really objected to this, and as he said at one point,

00:41:43.000 --> 00:41:47.570
if we are going to turn the Philippines back to the Filipinos, then we should turn Arizona

00:41:47.570 --> 00:41:49.110
back to the Apaches.

00:42:02.720 --> 00:42:07.910
As the summer wore on, Americans waited anxiously for news from Peking.

00:42:07.910 --> 00:42:14.700
The siege was now in its second month.

00:42:14.700 --> 00:42:20.020
Then on August 13th, after more than a month of fierce fighting, the International Army

00:42:20.020 --> 00:42:26.790
at last reached the Chinese capital to be met by the sickening stench of unburied dead.

00:42:31.630 --> 00:42:35.780
The Chinese had dug trenches around the city wall,

00:42:35.780 --> 00:42:39.000
and controlled all the gates.

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:53.450
The next morning, the international forces attacked.

00:42:53.450 --> 00:42:56.400
They stormed the walls.

00:43:00.060 --> 00:43:09.990
broke through the gates and routed the Chinese.

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:12.830
After 55 days of fear and waiting,

00:43:12.830 --> 00:43:19.240
the siege of the foreign compound was lifted.

00:43:19.240 --> 00:43:20.710
The hostages freed.

00:43:35.340 --> 00:43:38.240
That same day, 300 miles to the south,

00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:42.470
Eva and Charles Price prepared to leave with their daughter, Florence.

00:43:42.470 --> 00:43:49.680
They received word that 20 soldiers were coming to escort them to Peking.

00:43:49.680 --> 00:43:55.050
They were very leery, they were frightened, but they had no choice.

00:43:55.050 --> 00:43:58.420
Two weeks before, Eva had written her final letter.

00:44:00.090 --> 00:44:07.630
Dear homefolks, during the past six weeks we have lived from day to day expecting it to be our last

00:44:07.630 --> 00:44:17.590
on earth. 33 of our friends were beheaded. Among them were 12 children and two pregnant women.

00:44:17.590 --> 00:44:24.630
May God increase our faith and trust, our peace and willingness to do His will,

00:44:24.630 --> 00:44:30.000
with a heart full of love to you all from us three. Charles,

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:42.930
Eva and Florence.

00:44:42.930 --> 00:44:48.120
On the morning of the 15th, the Chinese soldiers brought a wagon to the compound and ordered

00:44:48.120 --> 00:44:49.520
the prices to get in.

00:44:49.520 --> 00:44:59.640
Before they left that day, she hugged Florence, and Florence did not know exactly what was

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:00.000
ahead.

00:45:02.010 --> 00:45:06.520
So when Florence left the room, Aunt Eva got down on her knees and prayed that if it was

00:45:06.520 --> 00:45:12.870
going to happen, that Florence would be taken first before she would be taken.

00:45:12.870 --> 00:45:15.530
And they were loaded in the wagon.

00:45:15.530 --> 00:45:24.630
It was a beautiful morning, such a beautiful morning that they sang going part of the way.

00:45:24.630 --> 00:45:28.870
They left the compound because they were assured that they would be given security.

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:33.840
And they were tricked.

00:45:33.840 --> 00:45:36.520
After a journey of just 20 miles,

00:45:36.520 --> 00:45:42.830
the Chinese soldiers drew their guns and their swords.

00:45:42.830 --> 00:45:50.580
They were hacked to death, and they were thrown in the ditch.

00:45:50.580 --> 00:45:54.200
And after it was all over, some of their Chinese friends

00:45:54.200 --> 00:45:57.040
went back and got their bodies and buried them.

00:46:03.130 --> 00:46:08.270
In Peking, some of the soldiers in the International Army had already begun to take revenge.

00:46:10.400 --> 00:46:13.060
But the hostages were free.

00:46:13.060 --> 00:46:28.400
Once more, the London Times exalted, a small segment of the civilized world, cut off and surrounded by an Asiatic horde, has exhibited those moral qualities which render mere numbers powerless.

00:46:35.240 --> 00:46:38.080
For nearly two months, the president had prayed daily

00:46:38.080 --> 00:46:40.860
that the hostages would be saved.

00:46:40.860 --> 00:46:42.540
Now, at the end of August,

00:46:42.540 --> 00:46:46.280
McKinley had one more cause for alarm.

00:46:46.280 --> 00:46:49.780
King Humbert I of Italy had just been assassinated

00:46:49.780 --> 00:46:53.500
in his summer palace by an anarchist.

00:46:53.500 --> 00:46:55.500
An anarchist had recently attempted

00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:58.390
to kill the Prince of Wales, the Shah of Persia.

00:47:00.380 --> 00:47:04.840
Now, they were targeting the President of the United States.

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:08.280
The anarchists were the international terrorists of their day.

00:47:08.280 --> 00:47:14.690
They went around killing heads of state or even just prominent people because they believed

00:47:14.690 --> 00:47:19.760
in some way this would bring the system down.

00:47:19.760 --> 00:47:25.620
They believed that the system is corrupt, that it's unfair to little people, and that

00:47:25.620 --> 00:47:29.640
if you lop off the head, then the body itself will die.

00:47:31.180 --> 00:47:35.480
The president was warned of the danger, but went about his life as he always had,

00:47:36.200 --> 00:47:41.840
returning to Canton, Ohio, to spend the summer with Ida, in the house they both so loved.

00:47:41.840 --> 00:47:48.180
We began our married life in that house, McKinley said.

00:47:48.770 --> 00:47:53.300
Our children were born there. One of them died and was buried from there.

00:47:54.690 --> 00:47:59.400
Some of the tenderest memories of my life are centered there, and some of the saddest.

00:48:02.750 --> 00:48:09.250
All that summer, McKinley tried to spend as much time with Ida as he could.

00:48:09.250 --> 00:48:11.530
Ida desperately needed her husband.

00:48:11.530 --> 00:48:16.460
She lived in constant fear of the violent seizures that threatened to overcome her.

00:48:16.460 --> 00:48:22.420
And they would come on suddenly. They would be playing cards or dining out with friends or something.

00:48:22.420 --> 00:48:26.100
And there was a hissing noise. That was always the warning sign.

00:48:26.100 --> 00:48:30.000
There was a loud hissing through her teeth, and he knew it was coming on.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.960
So he would throw either his handkerchief

00:48:31.960 --> 00:48:34.540
over her head to spare her embarrassment,

00:48:34.540 --> 00:48:38.180
or a napkin, whichever, if they were at the dining table.

00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:39.960
- Ida took to knitting slippers,

00:48:39.960 --> 00:48:42.570
which she gave away to charities.

00:48:42.570 --> 00:48:45.250
It was said that she knit 3,500 pairs

00:48:45.250 --> 00:48:50.060
while she was first lady.

00:48:50.060 --> 00:48:53.160
She cherished the daily rides alone with her husband

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:58.030
on the lonely back roads outside of Kenton.

00:48:58.030 --> 00:49:00.000
On a small farm just 60 miles away,

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:05.830
away, Leon Shogosh was living with his parents.

00:49:05.830 --> 00:49:10.200
Twenty-eight-year-old Shogosh hated the American government.

00:49:10.200 --> 00:49:13.200
He believed that all rulers were the enemies of working people.

00:49:13.200 --> 00:49:20.200
He is the son of immigrants, but American-born, who had sort of knocked around.

00:49:20.200 --> 00:49:24.680
Leon would drift off and he would work at various jobs in cities and then he would come

00:49:24.680 --> 00:49:27.060
back to the farm.

00:49:27.060 --> 00:49:30.000
He was almost certainly mentally disturbed.

00:49:31.150 --> 00:49:33.350
Later, his family would recall

00:49:33.350 --> 00:49:37.050
that Leon had stayed up night after night

00:49:37.050 --> 00:49:40.240
reading accounts of King Humbert's assassination.

00:50:12.800 --> 00:50:15.360
Across America, the presidential campaign

00:50:15.360 --> 00:50:18.570
was the great fall entertainment.

00:50:18.570 --> 00:50:23.230
Never before had candidates campaigned so hard, so far,

00:50:23.230 --> 00:50:26.630
or spent so much money.

00:50:26.630 --> 00:50:30.000
William Jennings Bryan, just 40 years old, was a fire

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:35.000
crisscrossing the country, speaking six, eight, even 12 times a day.

00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:39.170
But the Republicans had their own young powerhouse,

00:50:39.170 --> 00:50:42.060
the 41-year-old vice presidential nominee.

00:50:42.060 --> 00:50:46.740
"I am as strong as a bull moose," Theodore Roosevelt told reporters.

00:50:46.740 --> 00:50:48.860
And no one doubted him.

00:50:48.860 --> 00:50:51.330
He becomes the sensation of the 1900 campaign.

00:50:51.330 --> 00:50:54.630
He can match William Jenner's Bryan as a campaigner.

00:50:54.630 --> 00:50:58.010
Bryan could campaign 18 hours a day, so could Theodore Roosevelt.

00:50:58.010 --> 00:51:00.000
Bryan could draw a big crowd,

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:04.240
Theodore Roswell could draw bigger crowds. Brian could excite crowds by what he said.

00:51:04.690 --> 00:51:12.270
Theodore Roswell could excite crowds by who he was. By September, the Republicans appeared to be

00:51:12.270 --> 00:51:20.320
taking the lead. With the economy strong, Brian was having a hard time drumming up enthusiasm for

00:51:20.320 --> 00:51:26.970
his anti-imperialist campaign. He can't get any fire going. He can't arouse the public.

00:51:26.970 --> 00:51:30.000
He's campaigning out in Kansas, and he's going on

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:35.500
on about how terrible it is and what a danger it is to the country that we have this empire

00:51:35.500 --> 00:51:40.610
that's going to drag us down. Supposedly, one farmer looked at another and said, "Price

00:51:40.610 --> 00:51:45.070
of hogs is 60 cents a pound. Guess we can stand it."

00:51:45.070 --> 00:51:50.870
The election just two months away, Bryan decided to change course. He began to attack

00:51:50.870 --> 00:51:55.600
McKinley for his alliance with big business, blasting the president for his support of

00:51:55.600 --> 00:52:00.000
the trusts, the giant corporations that were swallowing up many

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:03.170
of America's small businesses.

00:52:03.170 --> 00:52:05.500
The trusts were destroying competition,

00:52:05.500 --> 00:52:09.770
manipulating prices, buying and selling politicians.

00:52:09.770 --> 00:52:14.220
- Brian is appealing to the plain people.

00:52:14.220 --> 00:52:17.480
Brian is saying very clearly that the railroads,

00:52:17.480 --> 00:52:20.500
the manufacturers, the bankers, Wall Street,

00:52:20.500 --> 00:52:23.640
were all fleecing the common folk,

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:26.120
the farmers, the workers.

00:52:26.120 --> 00:52:30.000
Brian was a demon, a absolutely fool.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:36.420
frightening character for the upper classes, for the wealthy. And there are moments in

00:52:36.420 --> 00:52:43.250
1900 where fear ripples through the upper classes.

00:52:43.250 --> 00:52:50.080
But for all his fiery oratory, Bryant still faced an uphill battle. Prosperity was the

00:52:50.080 --> 00:53:00.000
Republican rallying cry. A full dinner pail, the party slogan. That September,

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:03.360
coal miners in the anthracite fields of Pennsylvania

00:53:03.360 --> 00:53:06.960
were threatening to walk off the job.

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:10.330
A strike could throw the economy into turmoil,

00:53:10.330 --> 00:53:13.820
give Brian one last chance.

00:53:13.820 --> 00:53:15.180
- This can shut down the factories.

00:53:15.180 --> 00:53:18.320
This can shut the productive manufacturing side

00:53:18.320 --> 00:53:21.440
of the American economy down fast.

00:53:21.440 --> 00:53:24.260
The other thing is that anthracite coal

00:53:24.260 --> 00:53:26.770
was the chief home heating fuel.

00:53:26.770 --> 00:53:30.000
So that a miner's strike could bring a long,

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:35.550
winter. It's the kind of wild card that no politician and particularly not

00:53:35.550 --> 00:53:40.910
somebody who is as commanding and controlling as William McKinley ever

00:53:40.910 --> 00:53:46.870
wants to see happen. Throughout 1900 there had been ugly labor disputes

00:53:46.870 --> 00:53:53.070
everywhere. Bricklayers and carpenters, cigar makers and brewers, stone masons,

00:53:53.070 --> 00:53:58.770
pod carriers, mill hands, all were demanding better working conditions and higher pay.

00:54:02.940 --> 00:54:07.820
In the Pennsylvania coal fields, the president of the United Mine Workers had been trying for

00:54:07.820 --> 00:54:15.790
more than a year to organize the miners. John Mitchell was only 30 years old. He wore a jeweled

00:54:15.790 --> 00:54:21.670
ring and a Prince Albert suit, but the miners liked and trusted him. He was one of their own.

00:54:21.670 --> 00:54:28.130
His father had been a miner, and Mitchell himself had begun work in the mines when he was just a boy.

00:54:37.160 --> 00:54:44.860
child labor is common practice the average miner is making about 400 a year it wasn't enough to

00:54:44.860 --> 00:54:52.390
support a family consequently when you were nine ten years old pulled out of school whether you

00:54:52.390 --> 00:54:57.010
liked it or not and off you went into a place called the breaker which is a huge factory for

00:54:57.010 --> 00:54:58.610
the processing of coal

00:55:02.650 --> 00:55:08.670
and there the boys would sit and coal would come down and shoots underneath them they couldn't wear

00:55:08.670 --> 00:55:13.710
gloves because they had to go into the coal by hand and pick out the slate and the rock from

00:55:13.710 --> 00:55:20.510
the coals that came roaring down under them and they had bosses working behind them with bull

00:55:20.510 --> 00:55:27.050
whips and there was so much dust in the breaker that the kids would wear bandanas across their

00:55:27.050 --> 00:55:30.000
faces and they'd take them off and all you'd see was just a little white

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:46.070
Mitchell knew firsthand the misery of mining,

00:55:46.070 --> 00:55:49.180
but he also knew how difficult it would be

00:55:49.180 --> 00:55:56.300
to unite a divided workforce: Welsh, Slavs, Italians, Poles, Irish.

00:55:56.300 --> 00:56:00.000
Mitchell's main challenge was that the workforce is different

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:03.400
divided along ethnic lines, and there's deep hatred.

00:56:03.400 --> 00:56:07.770
When the Slavic miners were brought into the region, they were brought in, it was believed,

00:56:07.770 --> 00:56:11.510
to depress wages, and they got a great American welcome when they came off the train.

00:56:11.510 --> 00:56:14.880
They were stoned by the Irish miners.

00:56:14.880 --> 00:56:19.060
John Mitchell had an exceedingly difficult task ahead of him, one that had frustrated

00:56:19.060 --> 00:56:23.160
every other organizer beginning back in the 1850s.

00:56:23.160 --> 00:56:27.940
Every union movement had failed because of these ethnic divisions and differences.

00:56:27.940 --> 00:56:30.000
And a lot of his advisors--

00:56:30.030 --> 00:56:33.370
him that he was facing an absolutely impossible task.

00:56:33.370 --> 00:56:58.100
The summer heatwave lingered into September across much of the country.

00:57:00.760 --> 00:57:06.240
In Galveston, Texas, the beaches drew tourists from as far away as Kansas City.

00:57:06.240 --> 00:57:13.460
Down at the Pagoda Bathhouse, bathers were enjoying the heavy surf.

00:57:13.460 --> 00:57:20.340
Few paid any heed to the report on September 4th that a hurricane was building out in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:57:20.340 --> 00:57:30.000
Galveston was a popular vacation destination. They had music and music.

00:57:30.030 --> 00:57:36.570
public parks, there was even a man who invented a buggy with a sail on it who sailed along

00:57:36.570 --> 00:57:38.730
the beach.

00:57:38.730 --> 00:57:44.420
Despite daily warnings about the storm, it was business as usual down at Galveston's

00:57:44.420 --> 00:57:46.900
Deepwater Harbor.

00:57:46.900 --> 00:57:49.860
More than a hundred ships lined the stone-capped piers.

00:57:49.860 --> 00:57:58.260
Galveston was really the main hub of commerce in the entire state of Texas.

00:58:02.490 --> 00:58:07.710
It was the main export for almost all of the Texas crops, particularly cotton.

00:58:07.710 --> 00:58:14.180
It was one of the most prosperous cities in the entire country.

00:58:14.180 --> 00:58:18.400
Some people even called it a second New York City.

00:58:18.400 --> 00:58:23.930
Washed by Galveston Bay on one side and the Gulf of Mexico on the other, Galveston was

00:58:23.930 --> 00:58:29.670
a city of 36,000, sitting precariously on a narrow sand island.

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:37.610
only a few feet above sea level Galveston Island is a barrier island it

00:58:37.610 --> 00:58:42.750
sits out there and protects the mainland from the power of storm surge it is a

00:58:42.750 --> 00:58:51.400
barrier against hurricanes and nothing at all protects Galveston there had been

00:58:51.400 --> 00:58:57.090
talk of building a wall against the sea along the ocean shore but a seawall was

00:58:57.090 --> 00:59:00.000
expensive and a bond proposed to raise the money

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:09.170
never even came up for a vote the people of this island denied the power of that

00:59:09.170 --> 00:59:15.590
Gulf of Mexico out there for years and years they respected the Gulf but they

00:59:15.590 --> 00:59:22.010
were not afraid of it the 10 to 12 foot natural sand dunes that had been on the

00:59:22.010 --> 00:59:26.360
front of Galveston had been removed so tourists could have easier access to the

00:59:26.360 --> 00:59:30.000
beach so one of the natural barriers was gone

00:59:31.180 --> 00:59:35.610
They thought they could write out anything, anything.

00:59:40.630 --> 00:59:45.150
On September 8th, the head of the National Weather Service in Galveston woke early

00:59:45.150 --> 00:59:47.830
and was down at the beach by 5 a.m.

00:59:48.870 --> 00:59:51.530
Isaac Klein didn't like what he saw.

00:59:52.580 --> 00:59:56.440
The storm's swells were increasing in magnitude and frequency, he later wrote.

00:59:57.410 --> 00:59:58.930
Great danger was approaching.

01:00:03.900 --> 01:00:06.850
Klein was a careful, meticulous man.

01:00:06.850 --> 01:00:10.830
He lived with his family in a house he had built near the beach, which he thought could

01:00:10.830 --> 01:00:14.380
withstand any storm.

01:00:14.380 --> 01:00:18.940
He was unaccustomed to rash judgments.

01:00:18.940 --> 01:00:26.250
When he telegraphed the weather bureau in Washington, he tried to restrain his mounting anxiety.

01:00:26.250 --> 01:00:30.000
Unusually heavy swells from southeast.

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:36.830
Such high water with opposing winds never observed previously.

01:00:36.830 --> 01:00:41.450
But that day in downtown Galveston, no one paid much attention to warnings about the

01:00:41.450 --> 01:00:43.460
weather.

01:00:43.460 --> 01:00:49.110
It was a Saturday morning and many people worked at least half a day that morning.

01:00:49.110 --> 01:00:53.990
I remember my grandfather telling me about playing in the surf and how grand it was to

01:00:53.990 --> 01:00:56.560
see the waves smashing on the beach.

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:08.350
As the wind increased hour by hour, the tide inch by inch, Klein began to fear the worst.

01:01:08.350 --> 01:01:16.280
At noon, he hurried to the beachfront, warning everyone that great danger threatened.

01:01:16.280 --> 01:01:20.680
He went up and down the beach, telling people, seek higher ground, move into the heart of

01:01:20.680 --> 01:01:22.270
the city.

01:01:22.270 --> 01:01:27.670
But for the residents of Galveston, keep in mind, higher ground is very relative.

01:01:27.670 --> 01:01:30.000
Along the seafront, it was two and a half feet above sea level.

01:01:30.700 --> 01:01:36.190
downtown four and a half feet and somewhere out in the gulf was a 20-foot

01:01:36.190 --> 01:01:40.820
tidal surge hitting cool force for Galveston by the middle of the

01:01:40.820 --> 01:01:47.550
afternoon winds gusted up to 60 miles an hour the tide had reached eight feet

01:01:47.550 --> 01:01:54.130
bridges washed out trains stopped running there was no longer any way to

01:01:54.130 --> 01:02:00.000
leave the island an awful disaster

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:08.090
Klein wrote, was upon us. At three o'clock, Klein reported to Washington one last time.

01:02:09.100 --> 01:02:17.830
Gulf rising, water covers streets of about half city. Then the lines from Galveston fell silent.

01:02:18.850 --> 01:02:23.570
The city perched on the edge of the sea was entirely cut off from the outside world.

01:02:34.760 --> 01:02:43.600
galveston reeled as the winds punished its unprotected shores the sea rose higher and higher

01:02:43.600 --> 01:02:51.620
flooding the downtown reaching as high as a man's chest estimates of the wind speed

01:02:51.620 --> 01:03:00.000
were 120 miles an hour and upwards that's the kind of wind that will drive a straw through a pond

01:03:00.030 --> 01:03:07.680
tree but if it's full of sand we'll braid the hide right off of you klein left the telegraph office

01:03:07.680 --> 01:03:14.240
and fought his way home i waited nearly two miles to my home through water often above my waist he

01:03:14.240 --> 01:03:21.420
wrote hurricane winds were driving timbers and slates roofs of buildings were flying through the air

01:03:22.850 --> 01:03:29.670
around 7 30 a great tidal surge hit the south side of the city

01:03:30.190 --> 01:03:33.230
It knocked buildings off of their foundations.

01:03:33.230 --> 01:03:37.390
And houses fell upon houses and whole blocks of houses.

01:03:37.390 --> 01:03:41.470
The rapid rise of water drove Isaac and his family

01:03:41.470 --> 01:03:45.230
higher and higher up to the second level of their home.

01:03:45.230 --> 01:03:47.490
And there they could see all of the debris

01:03:47.490 --> 01:03:50.270
being whipped around by the water.

01:03:50.270 --> 01:03:53.550
Suddenly, a quarter-mile section of trolley track

01:03:53.550 --> 01:03:57.620
tossed loose by the storm slammed into the Klein home.

01:04:00.090 --> 01:04:04.620
The house creaked, Kline wrote, and was carried over into the surging waters.

01:04:05.470 --> 01:04:10.650
With my wife and six-year-old childhood, I was carried down under the wreckage.

01:04:13.910 --> 01:04:17.360
My wife's clothing was entangled, and she never rose again.

01:04:22.440 --> 01:04:27.120
A flash of lightning revealed my baby girl floating on wreckage a few feet away.

01:04:27.120 --> 01:04:29.440
I struggled out of the timbers.

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:55.000
Kline and his daughter drifted in the darkness for hours, enduring the fury of the storm.

01:04:55.000 --> 01:05:00.000
Sunday, September 9th, came with a clear sky, Kline remembered.

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:09.360
Just a calm and quiet sea, a most beautiful day, but oh, the horrible sights that greeted

01:05:09.360 --> 01:05:11.490
our eyes.

01:05:38.640 --> 01:05:42.140
nearly half of the homes of Galveston had been swept away.

01:05:45.540 --> 01:05:51.490
One area where 20,000 people had lived, not a house remained.

01:05:56.780 --> 01:06:00.000
Galveston was like a war zone that had been bombed.

01:06:04.700 --> 01:06:16.010
All the homes along the Galveston beachfront had been splintered, overturned.

01:06:16.010 --> 01:06:22.010
Galveston lost between 6,000 and 7,000 people out of a population of 36,000.

01:06:22.010 --> 01:06:28.300
That's one in six.

01:06:28.300 --> 01:06:30.000
The day after the storm,

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:35.700
the bell for people who died until they reached the point where they would be

01:06:35.700 --> 01:06:59.700
constantly tolling the bell so they stopped nothing but wreckage and bodies

01:07:03.420 --> 01:07:09.690
It was a sandbar piled high with death and full of destruction.

01:07:12.350 --> 01:07:16.070
It had been the worst natural disaster in the nation's history.

01:07:16.070 --> 01:07:22.280
The first reports from Galveston spoke of a city lifeless and bloomless,

01:07:23.430 --> 01:07:25.570
streets choked with debris and corpses,

01:07:26.830 --> 01:07:29.490
the vomitings of a maddened, retching ocean.

01:07:33.550 --> 01:07:38.830
Nine days after the storm, Clara Barton and the Red Cross arrived to tend the wounded and the

01:07:38.830 --> 01:07:47.920
homeless. Aid poured in from all over the country. As soon as he got word, President McKinley ordered

01:07:47.920 --> 01:07:55.450
troops to Galveston with tents and emergency supplies. But in the face of the overwhelming

01:07:55.450 --> 01:08:00.000
disaster, even as they grieved, the people of Galveston began

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:07.020
to rebuild. The bridges would be higher, the buildings stronger, the wharves

01:08:07.020 --> 01:08:12.400
reconstructed, as one resident said, to withstand even such a hurricane as the

01:08:12.400 --> 01:08:19.400
one we have just experienced. The people of this little sandbar showed a great

01:08:19.400 --> 01:08:30.000
deal of grit and determination. They rebuilt, they came back, they tried to bring

01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:37.400
bring this little city together again.

01:08:37.400 --> 01:08:43.640
- There was a tremendous feeling of optimism

01:08:43.640 --> 01:08:50.930
and wanting to build back and to reclaim their city.

01:08:50.930 --> 01:09:00.000
And it's a source of great pride to those who stayed here.

01:09:00.440 --> 01:09:05.230
Isaac Klein was able to file his formal report on the hurricane.

01:09:05.230 --> 01:09:11.260
He concluded, "It appears that a seawall would have broken the swells and saved much

01:09:11.260 --> 01:09:15.180
of the loss of both life and property."

01:09:15.180 --> 01:09:19.920
Isaac Klein recommended that something had to be done to protect the city from catastrophic

01:09:19.920 --> 01:09:23.130
storms in the future.

01:09:23.130 --> 01:09:27.130
What they had thought about years ago was what he thought they should do now.

01:09:27.130 --> 01:09:28.640
They should build that seawall.

01:09:31.660 --> 01:09:34.260
Nothing had ever been done like this before.

01:09:34.260 --> 01:09:47.800
Three miles long, 100 feet, 16.5 feet high.

01:09:47.800 --> 01:10:00.000
Galveston still sits precariously on a sandbar.

01:10:00.440 --> 01:10:05.440
protected by the seawall built after the most destructive hurricane in the nation's history,

01:10:05.440 --> 01:10:07.970
the hurricane of 1900.

01:10:35.560 --> 01:10:40.140
Two weeks after the Galveston hurricane, on September 17th,

01:10:40.580 --> 01:10:43.400
in coal towns throughout northeastern Pennsylvania,

01:10:44.320 --> 01:10:47.200
the United Mine Workers issued a call for a strike,

01:10:48.030 --> 01:10:51.090
demanding recognition of their union and a living wage.

01:10:53.810 --> 01:11:00.000
But on the morning of the strike, when the work whistle blew,

01:11:00.640 --> 01:11:07.170
No one knew, not the press, the White House, or even the Union, what the miners would do.

01:11:07.170 --> 01:11:12.430
Union President John Mitchell was asking bitterly divided miners to challenge the mightiest

01:11:12.430 --> 01:11:18.940
monopoly in America, a handful of wealthy coal operators controlled by America's most

01:11:18.940 --> 01:11:24.370
powerful financier, J. Pierpont Morgan.

01:11:24.370 --> 01:11:30.000
By 1900, J.P. Morgan Enterprises had just about wrapped up the entire anthracite region.

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:33.740
So you're fighting a distant corporation way off on Wall Street.

01:11:33.740 --> 01:11:35.970
And how do you reach out to that?

01:11:35.970 --> 01:11:38.130
How do you reach out even to communicate with it?

01:11:38.130 --> 01:11:41.530
And it's the largest industrial conglomeration,

01:11:41.530 --> 01:11:44.090
the largest industrial consolidation in the country,

01:11:44.090 --> 01:11:45.890
this anthracite empire.

01:11:45.890 --> 01:11:49.050
The miners feared the coal operators,

01:11:49.050 --> 01:11:51.790
but they distrusted one another even more.

01:11:51.790 --> 01:11:56.480
The men came from different countries, spoke different languages,

01:11:56.480 --> 01:12:00.000
and Mitchell had spent years trying to unite them.

01:12:00.890 --> 01:12:03.450
He had sent in teams of organizers

01:12:03.450 --> 01:12:06.190
before he arrives in the Antisite region,

01:12:06.190 --> 01:12:08.870
and they worked inside the community,

01:12:09.250 --> 01:12:10.730
learning the languages of the people,

01:12:10.890 --> 01:12:13.750
appreciating what they ate, appreciating their customs.

01:12:14.530 --> 01:12:17.160
And when he spoke to miners, it was this.

01:12:17.820 --> 01:12:20.940
You don't have to drink if you're Irish with Italians,

01:12:21.100 --> 01:12:22.180
but you work with them.

01:12:22.420 --> 01:12:24.580
And to get anything done in the way of improvements

01:12:24.580 --> 01:12:27.640
in the workforce at the job, at the job site,

01:12:27.840 --> 01:12:30.000
you've got to bury...

01:12:30.060 --> 01:12:34.980
your antagonism temporarily and join with these people in a common effort.

01:12:34.980 --> 01:12:40.430
Otherwise, you know, you're just fodder, cannon fodder for the capitalists.

01:12:40.430 --> 01:12:42.330
He had a refrain.

01:12:42.330 --> 01:12:44.300
Everywhere he went, it was the same.

01:12:44.300 --> 01:12:48.000
The coal you mine is not Slavic coal.

01:12:48.000 --> 01:12:50.090
It's not Irish coal.

01:12:50.090 --> 01:12:51.890
It's not Polish coal.

01:12:51.890 --> 01:12:54.080
It's not Italian coal.

01:12:54.080 --> 01:12:56.600
It's coal.

01:13:03.040 --> 01:13:08.440
By the day of the strike, Mitchell had convinced 9,000 miners to join the union,

01:13:08.440 --> 01:13:13.080
but more than 140,000 miners had not.

01:13:13.080 --> 01:13:17.440
Unless most of them went on strike, the union would be broken,

01:13:17.440 --> 01:13:23.490
the union men thrown out of their homes and fired, never to work in the mines again.

01:13:23.490 --> 01:13:28.770
In one home after another, miners and their wives struggled with a difficult decision.

01:13:30.800 --> 01:13:36.990
At dawn in the little coal towns across northeastern Pennsylvania, every miner wondered what his

01:13:36.990 --> 01:13:45.740
neighbor would do.

01:13:45.740 --> 01:13:50.230
Then slowly, men began to drift from their homes in their Sunday bests.

01:14:00.860 --> 01:14:11.730
Atonishment of everyone 90,000 men stayed out of the mines that first day some marched from one mind to the next

01:14:12.370 --> 01:14:18.270
Urging those who were still at work to come out and join them. These are not radical people

01:14:18.790 --> 01:14:25.810
They're fighting for a piece of the American pot in a sense so little a decent living wage

01:14:26.530 --> 01:14:30.000
Continue to support their churches support their communities

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:31.180
and support their families.

01:14:32.240 --> 01:14:33.850
By week's end,

01:14:33.990 --> 01:14:36.810
120,000 miners had joined the strike.

01:14:37.420 --> 01:14:38.720
By the second week,

01:14:38.860 --> 01:14:41.380
well over 90% of the mines were closed.

01:14:42.010 --> 01:14:44.780
But the mine owners refused to negotiate.

01:14:46.720 --> 01:14:49.980
The strike continued.

01:14:50.400 --> 01:14:51.700
Mitchell was becoming a hero,

01:14:52.040 --> 01:14:54.000
especially to the boys in whose ranks

01:14:54.000 --> 01:14:55.040
he had once stood,

01:14:55.830 --> 01:14:57.000
the Breaker boys.

01:14:58.350 --> 01:15:00.000
As I saw,

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:07.680
those eager eyes peering at me, he said, the fight had a new meaning for me. I felt that I was fighting

01:15:07.680 --> 01:15:18.200
for the boys, fighting a battle for innocent childhood. By the middle of October, the miners

01:15:18.200 --> 01:15:24.990
had been out for a month. They had no savings and little help from the union. Some were reduced to

01:15:24.990 --> 01:15:29.380
scavenging the woods for food. Still, they held on.

01:15:30.000 --> 01:15:34.020
- The miners at times were actually starving.

01:15:34.020 --> 01:15:35.860
They couldn't buy from the company store

01:15:35.860 --> 01:15:38.780
because they didn't have the credit.

01:15:38.780 --> 01:15:41.670
You had to mine the coal to get the credit.

01:15:41.670 --> 01:15:44.930
And the big companies, the companies that owned the mines,

01:15:44.930 --> 01:15:45.850
could care less.

01:15:45.850 --> 01:15:48.510
They thought, well, we'll starve them out.

01:15:48.510 --> 01:15:51.780
If they're going to strike, we'll stop it.

01:15:51.780 --> 01:15:53.880
They'll either work or else.

01:15:55.450 --> 01:15:56.870
- As the strike wore on,

01:15:56.870 --> 01:16:00.000
mills, factories, and homes across the country

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:09.090
were running low on coal, and prices began to skyrocket. With the election and cold weather

01:16:09.090 --> 01:16:12.750
coming on fast, the strike had become a campaign issue.

01:16:12.750 --> 01:16:18.570
Mitchell timed the strike perfectly. He timed the strike to occur just before the

01:16:18.570 --> 01:16:24.730
national election, to make a fool out of McKinley, with his campaign slogan of a full dinner

01:16:24.730 --> 01:16:30.000
pail. I mean, you can't even heat your homes in New York City and Philadelphia and places

01:16:30.030 --> 01:16:37.630
that. Also, you throw the issue to William Jennings Bryan. Bryan's running a campaign

01:16:37.630 --> 01:16:44.140
against these large industries that are wrapping up the economy. With the union and the coal

01:16:44.140 --> 01:16:50.720
operators stalemated, McKinley grew more and more concerned. He's especially worried as the

01:16:50.720 --> 01:16:54.680
weather gets colder and people's homes are going to start getting cold and that maybe

01:16:54.680 --> 01:17:00.000
people will blame him or blame the Republicans. So what he does is he

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:06.840
has his friend, his political colleague, Senator Mark Hanna, meet with the mine owners.

01:17:07.720 --> 01:17:13.010
Hanna approaches the owners, and he lays it right on the line. If this strike is successful,

01:17:13.010 --> 01:17:18.390
it's going to be Brian rather than McKinley, and you're in some real hot water at that point,

01:17:18.390 --> 01:17:21.830
because this is the guy who's going after the major monopolies and corporations.

01:17:21.830 --> 01:17:25.110
You people are running one of the most tightly knit and far-reaching

01:17:25.110 --> 01:17:30.000
industrial monopolies in the history of the world, and you don't want Brian in the presidency.

01:17:30.280 --> 01:17:32.340
You want Mr. McKinley.

01:17:32.340 --> 01:17:37.870
But despite the pressure from Hannah, the mine owners still refuse to bargain.

01:17:37.870 --> 01:17:43.630
When they refuse, he goes over their heads to Pierpont Morgan.

01:17:43.630 --> 01:17:49.150
And Morgan puts heavy pressure on, and all of a sudden, signs begin to appear that, yes,

01:17:49.150 --> 01:17:55.540
we're open for work at an increase of 5 and 6 percent of the wage scale, but no recognition

01:17:55.540 --> 01:17:56.540
of the union.

01:17:56.540 --> 01:18:00.000
That's one thing they did not do and wouldn't do.

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:07.620
the offer. But on October 28th, just one week before the election, the mine owners made

01:18:07.620 --> 01:18:14.610
a new proposal. They still refused to recognize the union, but they now offered a 10% increase

01:18:14.610 --> 01:18:25.120
in pay. Mitchell accepted. The strike was over. What they offer is hardly anything.

01:18:25.120 --> 01:18:30.000
But the miners are bankrupt. They're starving. And they're willing to go out. They're willing

01:18:30.030 --> 01:18:36.870
back to work because they have to go back to work to support their families they got it in a sense

01:18:36.870 --> 01:18:43.630
a de facto recognition of the union that it existed that mitchell existed that the umw was there and

01:18:43.630 --> 01:18:50.240
there for the long term but believe me you know this thing wasn't over because it's just a dress

01:18:50.240 --> 01:19:00.000
rehearsal for something bigger in the fall of 1900 john mitchell had done what many thought impossible uniting a divided

01:19:00.000 --> 01:19:07.880
group of workers. When Mitchell leaves the region, he leaves a workforce of over 100,000 men organized

01:19:07.880 --> 01:19:15.010
in a national union. In every worker's home after the 1900 strike, there would generally be a picture

01:19:15.010 --> 01:19:18.130
of the sacred heart of Jesus, and right beside it would be a picture of John Mitchell,

01:19:18.430 --> 01:19:25.260
Johnny DeMitch, they called him, our Lincoln, our savior. And when he leaves,

01:19:25.260 --> 01:19:30.000
the Breaker boys get together, and they give him a gold medallion, which he wears around his

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:37.000
And as his carriage leaves Hazleton, as many as 5,000 Breaker Boys followed it outside of town.

01:19:37.000 --> 01:19:51.020
With the miners' strike over, Brian had nothing left to run on.

01:19:51.020 --> 01:19:54.650
His attempt to label McKinley an imperialist had failed.

01:19:54.650 --> 01:19:58.860
So had his hopes to paint the president as a tool of big business.

01:20:00.090 --> 01:20:14.050
most Americans didn't seem to care.

01:20:14.050 --> 01:20:16.450
When the votes were counted, it was McKinley's picture

01:20:16.450 --> 01:20:26.500
that flashed on giant screens in cities across the country.

01:20:26.500 --> 01:20:29.220
Americans had voted their pocketbooks.

01:20:30.990 --> 01:20:36.570
Prosperity had carried the day, as it would so often throughout the coming centuries.

01:20:38.230 --> 01:20:42.690
The policy of the Republican Party, building an industrial powerhouse at home,

01:20:43.350 --> 01:20:47.100
expanding American power abroad, became the policy of the nation.

01:21:12.890 --> 01:21:15.490
But while most Republicans were celebrating,

01:21:16.280 --> 01:21:20.220
the Republican from North Carolina was being forced out of politics.

01:21:23.200 --> 01:21:25.920
Congressman George White had not run for re-election

01:21:25.920 --> 01:21:28.120
because he knew he could never win.

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:35.100
Across the South, black men had been kept from the polls by new laws, intimidation and

01:21:35.100 --> 01:21:36.930
violence.

01:21:36.930 --> 01:21:40.640
Even White's own family had been terrorized.

01:21:40.640 --> 01:21:47.150
In his farewell speech, White would rise to challenge his colleagues for the final time.

01:21:47.150 --> 01:21:54.140
It would be the last speech that any black would give to Congress for the next 28 years.

01:21:54.140 --> 01:22:00.000
These parting words, White said, are in behalf of an outraged, heartbroken, brood

01:22:00.000 --> 01:22:10.790
and bleeding people but industrious loyal people rising people full of potential this mr chairman

01:22:10.790 --> 01:22:20.090
is perhaps the negro's temporary farewell to the american congress but let me say phoenix-like

01:22:20.090 --> 01:22:30.000
he will rise up someday and come again yes

01:22:30.120 --> 01:22:39.800
it is a period of demoralization 1900 african americans they've had a bad time jim crow was

01:22:39.800 --> 01:22:49.790
there but they're not slaves the world is open to them and they want things to get better and

01:22:49.790 --> 01:22:56.180
they're working to make it better

01:23:01.720 --> 01:23:08.580
It's amazing that this song, Lift Every Voice and Sing, was composed in 1900.

01:23:08.580 --> 01:23:25.410
It has become the African-American national anthem.

01:23:31.500 --> 01:23:33.610
And what is there to sing about?

01:23:33.610 --> 01:23:38.510
Sing about the past, and sing about how far we have come.

01:23:38.510 --> 01:23:49.460
In spite of the oppression, lift your voices, and sing.

01:23:49.460 --> 01:23:51.910
It's a new century.

01:23:51.910 --> 01:23:54.740
This is 1900.

01:23:54.740 --> 01:23:58.520
This is a new day.

01:24:38.250 --> 01:24:44.280
As 1900 drew to a close, stories of change continued to herald a new era.

01:24:44.280 --> 01:24:52.450
In Boston on December 30th, the last horse-drawn trolley was replaced by an electric bus.

01:24:52.450 --> 01:24:59.860
That same week, the first overseas telephone call was made between Key West, Florida,

01:25:00.190 --> 01:25:09.180
Havana, Cuba, and on the last day of the 19th century, scientists reported they were searching

01:25:09.180 --> 01:25:30.000
for messages from Mars, a nation risen so fast.

01:25:30.160 --> 01:25:53.970
and gone so far as the United States had in the past 100 years to have everything.

01:26:14.590 --> 01:26:23.490
there was good reason for hope and confidence.

01:26:23.490 --> 01:26:26.910
It was more than the wonder of artificial light

01:26:26.910 --> 01:26:29.730
or ice in the summer and heat in the winter.

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:38.960
bath tubs with running water. The possibilities in America seemed greater than they'd ever been.

01:26:38.960 --> 01:26:59.800
Asking whether America had grown too rich and too powerful to live up to its own democratic ideals.

01:27:18.620 --> 01:27:24.520
At midnight on December 31st, 1900, Americans welcomed the 20th century.

01:27:30.510 --> 01:27:34.130
150,000 people gathered in downtown New York.

01:27:34.130 --> 01:27:41.830
Kansas City blazed with thousands of crimson incandescent lights.

01:27:41.830 --> 01:27:47.250
In town after town, newspapers and magazines were predicting marvelous things in the hundred

01:27:47.250 --> 01:27:49.980
years to come.

01:27:49.980 --> 01:27:53.600
Great airships would fly across the sea.

01:27:53.600 --> 01:27:57.690
Wireless telephones would span the world.

01:27:57.690 --> 01:28:00.000
People would watch moving images in their own homes.

01:28:01.560 --> 01:28:05.760
Someone, someday, would walk on the moon.

01:28:05.760 --> 01:28:08.480
There were even predictions that the 20th century

01:28:08.480 --> 01:28:17.850
would see an end to poverty and war.

01:28:17.850 --> 01:28:23.270
America was the richest and most powerful nation on Earth,

01:28:23.270 --> 01:28:26.150
a popular president who would lead the country

01:28:26.150 --> 01:28:29.640
into the new century, anything

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:48.690
On September 6, 1901, Leon Shogash made his way through the enormous crowds at the Pan-American

01:28:48.690 --> 01:28:53.220
Exposition in Buffalo, New York.

01:28:53.220 --> 01:28:57.890
Shogash was headed for the Temple of Music, where he would wait for the President of the

01:28:57.890 --> 01:28:58.930
United States.

01:29:00.000 --> 01:29:05.020
In his pocket was a short-barreled .32-caliber Ivor Johnson revolver.

01:29:05.020 --> 01:29:14.740
At seven minutes past four, as McKinley reached out to shake the hands of those who had come to greet him,

01:29:15.460 --> 01:29:16.840
Shogosh fired twice.

01:29:18.760 --> 01:29:20.800
The president fell wounded.

01:29:23.110 --> 01:29:26.070
For a week, McKinley's life hung in the balance.

01:29:29.270 --> 01:29:30.000
He called for a...

01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:38.160
Ida on the last day, Friday the 13th. As she bent over him and held his hands, he was heard

01:29:38.160 --> 01:29:46.640
to murmur, "Nearer my God to thee." Then she was helped from the room. "My precious

01:29:46.640 --> 01:29:53.620
left us this morning around two o'clock," Ida wrote in her diary. "I hope the dear Lord

01:29:53.620 --> 01:29:55.350
will take me very soon."

01:30:04.960 --> 01:30:19.430
As the country mourned, the Pan-American exposition dimmed its lights.

01:30:19.430 --> 01:30:24.560
Two days, the festival of progress, meant to herald the 20th century,

01:30:24.560 --> 01:30:27.590
stood like a silent monument to the dead president.

01:30:36.970 --> 01:30:41.550
Then, as the new president, young Theodore Roosevelt, made his way to Washington,

01:30:42.800 --> 01:30:49.080
the exposition reopened its doors, turned on its lights.

01:30:56.980 --> 01:30:58.960
There were fireworks over Lake Erie.

